<BaseLayout
  title="Disabled by Design"
  description="Disabled by Design — what the name means, who June is, and the design orientation behind the work."
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <h1 class="brand-title">Disabled <span class="brand-by">by</span> Design</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A structural critique, a design parameter, and a refusal.</p>
  </div>

  <section>
    <h2>What the Name Means</h2>
    <div class="about-content">
      <p>
        I design from the standpoint of a disabled trans Ethnic Studies scholar who taught on
        contingent faculty lines. Every one of those positions is a parameter — shaping what I perceive,
        what I build, and what I refuse to accept as given. They let me see things somebody with
        a different neurotype, a different body, a different institutional position would not
        have thought of. That's a feature, not a deficit.
      </p>
      <p>
        Disabled by Design also names the problem: systems are <em>designed</em> in ways that produce
        disability. Rigid deadlines, surveillance grading, deficit-model assessments — the
        infrastructure disables people, then calls the people broken. The social model of
        disability applied to every structure I study and build.
      </p>
      <p>
        And there's the third meaning: against the impulse to genetic-engineer away disability.
        I don't want a future that optimizes me out. My existence is an asset.
      </p>
      <p>
        Disabled knowledge can do more than identify that exclusion: it can change what a system is
        for, whose knowledge shapes it, and what gets built. I bring that practice into
        <a href="/consulting/">consulting</a> with justice-oriented organizations, carrying qualitative
        and critical inquiry into the systems they rely on. I draw distinctly—not interchangeably—on
        disability justice; feminist, queer and trans thought; anti-racist and anti-colonial analysis;
        Indigenous scholarship; and community-based research. Each makes different relations of power
        and possibility visible.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The Design Orientation</h2>
    <div class="about-content">
      <p>
        I don't just build software that accomplishes a task. I reinvent the concept behind
        the task — interrogate the power relations embedded in it, critique them, and build
        something that operates from different assumptions.
      </p>
      <p>
        In my teaching, I reinvented what an assignment could be — from an endpoint for
        evaluation to a launchpad for discussion. That conceptual shift made the work
        automatable in the small ways I needed it to be, and the automation opened up the
        pedagogy. Autograder4Canvas reads student work with a local LLM and surfaces patterns
        for me, the teacher; it doesn't grade. The prompts ask the model to describe what
        each student seems to be reaching for rather than score against a static rubric, to
        attempt to flag silencing patterns like respectability policing or recentering of
        whiteness, and to attempt to surface burnout signals. They also instruct the model
        to counter common LLM biases against minoritized students. Whether the model holds
        any of those instructions on a given run is an empirical question, not a guarantee.
        The model runs locally on the teacher's laptop, so student work is not sent to an external
        AI provider. Each piece feeds the other.
      </p>
      <p>
        I built one tool to help me do more praxis, and it helped me build prompt scaffolding
        for more praxis. Reframe wraps the LLM call in a 5-stage workflow that asks the
        model to engage with critical-theory framework specifications before responding —
        76 framework specifications, each authored to instruct the model to speak from a
        specific tradition before synthesis, surface tensions without flattening, and ask
        whose erasure is a structural condition of the conversation (extending Gayatri
        Spivak — probably impossible at the prompt-time layer, but worth the attempt). Of
        the 76, 8 currently have authored detection signatures the system can use to track
        engagement; the rest are specifications without instrumentation. The session prompts
        are designed for how I actually think: STEP for parallel infodump-style threads,
        PARK for tangents, WEAVE for through-lines across a session. A distributed
        intelligence prompt scaffold attempts to surface through-lines across sessions; that
        layer is partially implemented.
      </p>
      <p>
        The critique and the code are the same practice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Survival Architecture</h2>
    <div class="about-content">
      <p>
        Every tool I've built emerged from conditions that demanded it. Autograder4Canvas:
        built when I was teaching ~200 students across four sections without a TA, to read
        student work and surface patterns for me. EvalEye: built when the college wasn't
        processing student evaluations and the forms sat in boxes. RoboStripper: scholarly
        reading made accessible as audio when the ability to focus on reading degrades under
        political crisis. Reframe: prompt scaffolding for critical analysis under conditions
        of teaching under fascism. The interactions with the LLM were going to happen anyway;
        the question was whether the prompts could route them through critical theory before
        the model answered.
      </p>
      <p>
        The conditions that make me precarious are the same conditions that made this system
        necessary and possible.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
